sys:{
  title:'The system: a software factory', sub:'UniversalSystem — an in-house software production system',
  intro:'Instead of building a product, <b>I built a tool that builds products.</b> Picture a production line where each stage runs on its own and only waits for a human on the decisions that matter. That is what this system does for building software and games. The figures below come from a live health check of the running stack; the dashboard screenshot underneath is that check, unedited.',
  kpis:[['84,290','lines of code across 432 files'],['17 / 17','services live and healthy'],['385+','automated tests'],['11','AI services connected']],
  dashCap:'Real output of a live system check: all 17 services up and healthy, plus 11 independent health probes — all passing',
  svcTitle:'What is inside this factory?', svcSub:'every service, in plain words',
  svc:{'api-gateway':'The front door: every request arrives here first and is routed to the right place.',
    'python-ai':'The AI brain: generation of code, images, audio, music and 3D models.',
    'dotnet-api':'The core logic: business rules, users and product data.',
    'node-microservices':'Light side services: real-time tasks and device communication.',
    'maf-orchestrator':'The agent commander: splitting work between several AI assistants and tracking results.',
    'matchmaker':'Online match coordination: putting players together for multiplayer.',
    'celery-worker':'The background worker: heavy, slow jobs without keeping the user waiting.',
    'qdrant':'Long-term memory: semantic search across everything the project has learned.',
    'sqlserver':'The main database: safe, structured storage.',
    'redis':'Fast memory: instant answers for frequently used data.',
    'rabbitmq':'Message queue: jobs line up and none of them get lost.',
    'mosquitto':'Lightweight messaging for devices and sensors.',
    'prometheus':'The health guard: constant metrics from every part.',
    'grafana':'Visual dashboard: the whole system at a glance.',
    'jaeger':'The tracer: follows each request step by step so slowness can be found.',
    'proxy':'Traffic control between services.',
    'web-frontend':'The web interface people actually work with.',
    'vision-watcher':'The system’s eyes: it looks at visual output and reports what looks wrong.'},
  capTitle:'Key capabilities',
  caps:[{t:'Long-term project memory',d:'The system remembers everything built and every decision taken, so it never starts from zero and never breaks finished work.'},
    {t:'Smart provider management',d:'Connected to 11 different AI services; if one fails or runs out of quota it automatically moves to the next and work continues.'},
    {t:'Safety layer',d:'Dangerous commands that could delete project files are blocked — this part is covered by 22 dedicated tests.'},
    {t:'Self-healing',d:'When an error occurs the system reads it, finds the cause and attempts a fix itself.'},
    {t:'The system’s eyes',d:'It looks at the game’s visual output and reports visual defects — quality is not judged by code alone.'},
    {t:'Automated content and scene building',d:'Images, audio, music and 3D models generated from a text description, and automatic scene assembly — in one run the system placed over 176 objects across 30 sections inside the game engine.'},
    {t:'Direct AI-assistant integration',d:'Nine of the factory’s tools are exposed directly to an AI assistant for use without a middle layer.'},
    {t:'Documentation and a proof rule',d:'Nothing is called “done” without evidence: file name, line number and a successful run.'}],
  why:'<b>What the structure is for:</b> the repetitive parts of every project — scaffolding code, producing assets, running tests, writing documentation — were written once and are reused on the next project. The figures above are this system’s own output and also appear in the résumé; the system itself runs on a personal machine and is available for a live demo.'
},
